{
	title: 'Expand or collapse cards: /expand, /collapse, lists, live hint, Tab',
	spec: `/toggle, /expand and /collapse, and their dialog, on the terminal and the web alike. The user-facing term is "card"; its states are expanded and collapsed. The dialog title is "Expand or collapse cards".

Syntax (shared by all three commands): a list of items separated by commas, spaces or both (t14,t16,t30-45 and #t14 16 30-45 are the same). An item is an optional '#', optional kind letters, then a number, a range N-M (inclusive) or '*'. Kinds: t tool, r reasoning (thinking), a assistant, u user, m message from another session, s system, q question. Letters combine: au10-40 means the user and assistant cards in 10-40; t* means every tool card. A single number names one card of any kind; a letter before it is ignored (#t24, t24, 24 are the same). A range or '*' without letters never touches user or assistant cards; naming a or u includes them. An empty target is the latest tool card.

/expand expands every matched card and /collapse collapses them; neither name may be confused with /close, which closes the tab. /toggle flips one named card; for anything more (a range, '*' or a list) it applies the majority rule to the whole match: expand all when more than half are collapsed, otherwise collapse all; a tie expands, since expanding hides nothing. Fold state stays per client, so each client applies /toggle's rule to its own state.

Dialog (Ctrl-O, runs /toggle; footer "enter: toggle · tab: complete · esc: close"): the line "Which cards to expand or collapse?", a blank row, the field, flush with the box's left padding like every row below it (no indent, no prompt sign); a blank row; a live hint that updates on every keystroke and says what Enter does ("Expands 12 tool cards: t4-t88") or, in the warning color (yellow), why the input is invalid; a blank row; then a help area:
    t1905, 1905   one card      *       all cards
    1880-1905     a range        t*, r*  all of a kind
    t1890, t1905  a list         empty   latest tool card

    User and assistant cards change only when you name them (a, u).
    Kinds: tool, reasoning, assistant, user, message, system, question
  The examples use the session's real tool card ids: the latest (one card), the third latest to the latest (range) and the two latest (list). With fewer than two tool cards they fall back to 24, 10-24 and t14, t24. In the terminal, help descriptions start in one column, two spaces after the widest key, and wrapped lines continue in that column. The kinds row separates words with commas, like the terminal's key help row; each word's first letter (the kind letter) is bright and bold. The placeholder is static ("24, 10-24 or t*").

  Tab completes like /command completion: it fills the common prefix of the matching card ids for the item under the cursor; when several match it lists them in the help area, packed several per row, newest first, at least 10; more than fit end with "+N more". It never cycles.

Size: the terminal dialog is as tall as its content (field, hint, help or completions); the web dialog is as tall as its content too, not the full picker height.

Typed by the user (or Ctrl-O), the client runs the command itself, unrecorded. Run by the model through the command tool, the host resolves the target against history, tells the following clients to apply it, and returns the matched cards, each with its model and effort where history records them ("Expanded 3 thinking cards: r12 (opus-5-5, xhigh), r40 (opus-5-5, xhigh), r77 (opus-5-5, high)"), so the model can narrow a request such as "expand the xhigh thinking cards" into a list. /toggle's result says "Toggled" with the same list, since the outcome depends on each client's state.`,
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		"The parser and dialog logic live in src/common (toggle.ts: parse, resolve, majority rule; blocks-dialog.ts: live hint, Tab completion, packed id rows) so both clients share them; the host's src/host/session-blocks.ts gives /go and /toggle the same block list. Tab reuses completion.common from the /command completer rather than a copy.",
		'Web folds.set must use updater setters: /expand t* sets several blocks in one batch, and reading the signal inside untrack saw the pre-batch state, so only the last block expanded.',
		"The web compact Picker needs height: fit-content; height: auto still filled the dialog's fixed picker height.",
		'The /collapse description is collapse cards (t*, 10-24); the earlier "not /close" warning read as noise in completion.'
	],
	needs: ['ghs', 'r4d'],
	foldInto: ['ghs']
}
